                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Sudden Strike 4 is a solid tactical strategy game. It’s not a masterpiece, but it’s a decent time if you prefer methodical planning over frantic starcraft-style clicking. It captures that classic real-time tactics vibe, even if it has some quirks that keep it from being top-tier.\n\nLike a lot of other reviews say, the key to winning this game is belly-crawling and artillery. You basically take your scouts, drop them into a prone stance (where they look like those old wind-up toy soldiers), and slowly creep them forward to clear the fog of war. Once they spot the enemy, you let your long-range artillery do the work from safety. It’s a slow loop, but it works.\n\nDespite its issues, the game creates some unique combat moments. At one point in the Desert War DLC, I managed to hijack a functioning Italian self-propelled gun and turned it against them. In another moment, one of my tank shells bounced off the sloped front armor of a Panzer and deflected into a nearby German mortar team, wiping them out. When the physics and simulation align like that, the game works well.\n\nI prefer to play a game like this single-player, so I am currently making my way through the campaigns. Pushing through those historical theaters gives a decent sense of progression, and the bonus missions offer plenty of extra content.\n\nThe game isn't perfect, and it has some consistency issues. Playing as the Allies, it feels like almost every tank has the exact same firing range, no matter the size of the main gun. It looks odd when a tiny light scout vehicle is trading shots at the exact same distance as a massive Churchill heavy tank. Also, the missions are highly scripted. You can feel the hand of the developer triggering enemy counter-attacks the second your troops cross an arbitrary line.\n\nEven with the rigid scripting, beating these levels is satisfying. Pulling off an ambush, managing your limited ammo, and finally cracking open a tough defensive line is rewarding. If you have the patience for a slower, micromanagement-heavy pace, Sudden Strike 4 is worth picking up.\n\nRating: 7/10",
